1	// Test-only fault-injection shim, preloaded into the messagetag process by
2	// the regression suite (tests/regression_test.py). It serves two purposes.
3	//
4	// 1. Simulate a read(2) failure that happens *after* part of the message
5	//    file has already been read successfully. Neither a missing file nor an
6	//    unreadable-permission file can produce that situation: both fail at
7	//    open time. Overriding read() is what lets the tests reach the "open
8	//    succeeded, some bytes delivered, then the read failed" path
9	//    deterministically, without relying on the account's permissions or on
10	//    special filesystems.
11	//
12	// 2. Replay *recoverable* read trouble -- transient EINTRs interleaved with
13	//    bounded (short) reads, optionally ending in a permanent error -- which
14	//    the AFTER/ERRNO mode cannot express, so the suite can pin "read
15	//    interrupted, retried, and the complete message still authenticated",
16	//    including an interruption before the first message byte and one after a
17	//    non-empty prefix.
18	//
19	// Legacy behaviour is controlled through environment variables:
20	//   MESSAGETAG_READ_FAULT_PATH   absolute path of the file to fault on;
21	//                                every other file is read normally
22	//   MESSAGETAG_READ_FAULT_AFTER  number of bytes to deliver before the
23	//                                first failing read (must be > 0 so a
24	//                                non-empty prefix has already been read)
25	//   MESSAGETAG_READ_FAULT_ERRNO  optional errno for the failure
26	//                                (default EIO)
27	//
28	// The failing read returns -1 once the allowed prefix has been delivered;
29	// every subsequent read of that file fails the same way.
30	//
31	// The second, scripted mode is selected by a non-empty
32	// MESSAGETAG_READ_FAULT_SCRIPT and takes precedence over the legacy
33	// variables:
34	//   MESSAGETAG_READ_FAULT_FD      fd the script applies to (default 0 =
35	//                                 standard input); every other fd is read
36	//                                 normally. With "--file -" the test hands
37	//                                 the message file over as fd 0, so reads
38	//                                 are deterministic like any regular file.
39	//   MESSAGETAG_READ_FAULT_SCRIPT  comma-separated steps, consumed one per
40	//                                 read() of the target fd:
41	//                                   <N>    bound this read to at most N
42	//                                          bytes (an ordinary short read;
43	//                                          whatever the underlying read
44	//                                          returns -- some bytes, or 0 at
45	//                                          EOF -- is passed through)
46	//                                   PASS   leave this read completely
47	//                                          untouched (one read slot passes
48	//                                          through with its original count)
49	//                                   EINTR  fail this read with EINTR (the
50	//                                          caller is expected to retry)
51	//                                   EPIPE / ENOSPC / EIO
52	//                                          fail with that errno and keep
53	//                                          failing every later read the
54	//                                          same way (a permanent error)
55	//                                 Once the script is exhausted, reads pass
56	//                                 through to the real read() untouched.
57	//   MESSAGETAG_READ_FAULT_TRACE   optional file path; in script mode every
58	//                                 intercepted read appends one line to it:
59	//                                 "D<n>" (n bytes delivered), "E<n>" (read
60	//                                 failed with errno n) or "P<n>" (n bytes
61	//                                 delivered by a read that passed through
62	//                                 after the script ended). The suite uses
